WHATNOW

What tam-clam is doing now and what comes next. Read this first; revise
it at the end of every session (work/README-work).

Updated: 2026-11-23, with W10 (0.1.4)


STATE

  Version 0.1.4, Phase P2 (POSIX spec suite and fuzzing), on branch dev.
  It adds:
  - Oils-style POSIX.1-2024 spec suite in tests/spec/ (run-spec.py plus
    10 topic files, 92 cases) comparing tam-clam side-by-side against
    installed reference shells (bash --posix today; dash, mksh, and
    busybox ash auto-detected when installed);
  - two POSIX conformance fixes caught by the spec suite: IFS field
    splitting of unquoted literals inside ${x:-word}/${x:+word} in
    src/expand.c, and special_fail on readonly unset in non-interactive
    shells in src/builtins_special.c;
  - fuzzing targets in tests/fuzz/ (fuzz_parse.c, fuzz_expand.c,
    fuzz_main.c) with 24 seed corpus files in tests/fuzz/corpus/,
    wired into make test, make sanitize (corpus replay), and make fuzz
    (clang libFuzzer when installed, gcc ASan+UBSan mutation fuzzer
    otherwise);
  - sandbox/demo-0.1.4-spec-fuzz added to sandbox/play.
  readelf -d lists only libc.so.6. make test, make sanitize, make fuzz
  (10,000 ASan+UBSan mutations per target), and self-dogfooding
  tests/run-scripts.sh all pass.

  Fred's decisions so far are in ROADMAP.md and the plans: POSIX.1-2024
  is the target; lint mode is P2 0.1.5; tam-clam ships only its own key
  map (no built-in readline map), drafted in arch/keys.md and agreed with
  Fred before 0.2.0; FUNCNEST sets the function recursion limit; signed
  64-bit arithmetic overflow is a reported error; echo recognizes -n only
  and does not interpret backslash escapes by default.


